# Built shards are release assets, not repository content: they are reproducible from
# index.jsonl plus the fixture generators, and committing binaries defeats §7's "append is
# one new blob and one index line".
shards/
blobs/

# Downloads fetched by fetch-benign.sh. §6: commit the lockfile and the script, never the
# downloads - that is what makes the benign half reproducible rather than merely shipped.
benign/downloads/

# The local-only half of the index: rows for samples that are held and never published.
# Its counts are carried in the tracked index-summary.json, so the accounting survives
# without the rows - see SOURCES.md.
local/

# Lock files and in-flight temp files from indexio.py. Both are transient by
# construction - a stale .lock is a crashed writer, and a stale .tmp is a write
# that never reached its os.replace(). Neither is repository content.
*.lock
*.tmp

# Bytecode cache from importing indexio.py as a module.
__pycache__/

# The derived SQLite read index and any journal SQLite puts beside it. Built by
# derive-index-db.py from both halves of the index; one-way, rebuildable, never
# authoritative about anything.
#
# It is gitignored for the same reason local/ is, and this entry is deliberately
# redundant with the local/ rule above so that the reason is written where somebody
# looking for the database will find it: THE DATABASE CONTAINS CUSTOMER IDENTIFIERS.
# It holds every local row verbatim, so the local half's origin.path, account_hash and
# site values are inside it by construction. It is exactly as unpublishable as
# local/index-local.jsonl and it must never be force-added, uploaded, or attached to
# an issue. See SOURCES.md, "The derived SQLite read index".
local/index.db
local/index.db-journal
local/index.db-wal
local/index.db-shm
local/index.db.*.tmp

# Review sessions written by review-app.py: the proposals file assign-family.py consumes and
# the log every timing figure in a round report comes from.
#
# Covered already by the local/ rule above, and named here anyway for the same reason
# local/index.db is: THE SESSION FILES CONTAIN CUSTOMER IDENTIFIERS AND MALWARE. A proposal
# records the literals a reviewer picked out of sample bytes, and malware hardcodes its
# victim - so a marker can be a customer's own host. The log records which screens were
# opened, and a screen is named after a rule-set or a literal. review-app.py renders
# origin.path and account names on purpose, because a reviewer deciding what a file is needs
# to see where it sat; that is why it binds 127.0.0.1 and why it is never an artifact, a
# hosted page, or anything with a URL somebody else can reach.
#
# assign-family.py sweeps the recorded name, basis and markers with the same leak predicate
# pre-push-check.py uses, and refuses the write if one carries an identifier - but that
# guards the INDEX. Nothing guards these files except this line and the rule above.
local/review-sessions/
